<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-panel ifm-takeaways"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-panel ifm-takeaways"><!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Key points</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list {"className":"ifm-ticks"} -->
<ul class="wp-block-list ifm-ticks"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>SEO is sold three ways: by the hour, on a monthly retainer or as fixed-price plans.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Whichever you choose, ask what will actually be done and how you will see it.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Be wary of guaranteed positions, long contracts and prices that are never written down.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Start with the one job most likely to matter, then build from there.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Prices for SEO vary enormously, and the reason is simple: "SEO" is not one thing. It covers fixing a website, rewriting pages, writing articles, building links and more. Before comparing prices, it helps to understand the three ways the work is sold.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Hourly or day rates</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You pay for time, usually from a freelancer or consultant. This suits one-off advice, a specific problem or a second opinion. The risk is that you do not know the total in advance, so agree a limit and ask for a short written summary of what was done.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Monthly retainers</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You pay a fixed amount each month and the agency works on your site continuously. This is the traditional model and suits businesses that need steady, ongoing work. The risks are the ones small businesses complain about most: contracts of six or twelve months, vague monthly reports and no clear list of what was done.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Fixed-price plans</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You buy a defined piece of work at a published price: an audit, a set of pages improved, a number of directory listings, a month of defined tasks. You know the cost and the scope before you start. The limit is that a plan covers what it lists and nothing more, so it suits jobs that can be clearly described.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This is how we sell SEO. Our <a href="/services/local-seo/">Local SEO</a> plans start [ifm_from service="local-seo" prefix="from " fallback="at a fixed price"], and <a href="/services/monthly-seo/">Monthly SEO</a> is sold one month at a time.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What changes the price</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>The size of your site.</strong> Fifty pages take longer to check and improve than five.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>How competitive your market is.</strong> A solicitor in a city needs more work than a village cafe.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>How much is done for you.</strong> Advice costs less than hands-on work.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Content and links.</strong> Writing and placing them takes real time, so plans that include them cost more.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>The state of the site.</strong> A slow, broken site needs fixing before anything else will help.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

